WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:04.530
- Here is Denver, Colorado's

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:07.140
top fine dining establishment,

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.553
the Buckhorn Exchange.

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:16.560
It tells you everything you
need to know about the city,

00:00:17.886 --> 00:00:22.495
guns, whiskey, taxidermy.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.753
Yes, this is Denver in a nutshell.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:30.208
God bless the West.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.693
Okay, okay, the city is
actually nothing like this.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:40.023
It's mostly like this,

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.123
sunshine even in the winter,

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.140
stoners and non-stoners,

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.113
uniting in their celebration
of the great outdoors.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:55.793
Enjoy.

00:00:59.604 --> 00:01:00.604
- Go, D. Park.

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:21.780
- Oh, hi.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.370
I didn't see you there.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:24.990
Normally at this part of the program,

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:28.110
I would be walking through
the front door of a startup,

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:31.380
but on today's episode, we cannot do that

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.990
for this startup wants to remain secret

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:36.873
because its nerds are making a smart gun.

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:45.840
That Navy SEAL you see before you

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.273
is holding a gun made by Biofire.

00:01:49.548 --> 00:01:52.440
Its very, very big claim is
that it's the first company

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.800
to make a smart gun that actually works.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.530
That's right, James Bond style,

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.603
fingerprint and facial recognition.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:05.490
The gun only fires if the
right person is holding it.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:07.320
- It knows somebody's holding onto it

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.050
but no matter how much
he pulls the trigger,

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:12.950
nothing's going to happen because
the system is not unlocked

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.610
- The person who started
Biofire is Kai Kloepfer.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:19.320
He's only 25 years old,

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.620
and has been working on
making this gun for 10 years.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:24.330
A project he started shortly

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:26.790
after the Aurora theater shooting.

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:29.740
- At the time, it's like the
worst mass shooting in history

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.190
and happened right just down the road here

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:33.240
from where I grew up.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:36.270
I was fortunate enough to not have to

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:37.950
think about gun violence or gun deaths,

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.560
and that was really my
first, sort of, encounter

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:41.393
with the topic, right?

00:02:41.393 --> 00:02:42.720
I was just starting to grow up,

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.040
just starting to think
about these kind of topics

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:44.890
as a 15-year-old

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.070
and really starting to think about

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:49.203
how could we do something about this.

00:02:50.806 --> 00:02:52.770
I started thinking about this
concept of smart gun, right?

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:54.120
And by no means am I the first person

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.560
to think about this obviously

00:02:56.411 --> 00:02:57.690
and it's been in science fiction forever,

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:01.800
but nobody had ever really
built a working smart gun.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.840
- Okay, so you're 15

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.590
and instead of doing some policy,

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:07.560
you're inspired

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:08.393
- I was never a policy person.

00:03:08.393 --> 00:03:10.020
- Okay, you're like, "I'm an engineer."

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:11.347
- I've been forced to become
a little bit more of one,

00:03:11.347 --> 00:03:12.960
you know, over the last 10 years.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:13.890
But no, I was an engineer.

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:16.260
I was a socially awkward nerd.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:19.830
- A bonafide nerd who
clinched his nerd bonafides

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:22.560
by getting an award at
the very prestigious

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:26.340
Intel International Science
and Engineering Fair.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:27.895
So, okay, so for people who don't know,

00:03:27.895 --> 00:03:29.340
I mean, this is like no joke.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:32.940
This is like thousands of
the world's smartest kids.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:33.773
- Yeah, top.

00:03:33.773 --> 00:03:35.100
The year I win, it was the top 1,500

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:36.570
out of over 8 million people

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:37.735
who competed across the year.
- 8 million people, right?

00:03:37.735 --> 00:03:39.931
Yeah, like this is the top of the top.

00:03:39.931 --> 00:03:40.920
- Yeah, it's as high as it gets,

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:43.380
I think, for the Science Fair world.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:46.470
At the time, I was mostly
an electrical engineer

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:47.400
and I could sort of fake my way

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:48.420
through some mechanical engineering.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:49.980
So built a pretty good electrical system

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:52.020
and a really mechanical system,

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:52.980
sort of slapped them together

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:54.900
into this kind of gun-shaped object.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.100
Not an actual firearm in any way.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:57.630
I wasn't allowed to bring
a gun to the Science Fair,

00:03:57.630 --> 00:03:59.508
quite obviously.
- Right.

00:03:59.508 --> 00:04:01.470
- Like, I was very surprised by all this.

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:04.470
You know, I'd done a lot of
Science Fair project before,

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:05.303
Three-quarters of the time,

00:04:05.303 --> 00:04:06.900
people didn't believe that
I had actually built them.

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:08.955
They thought my parents had did them.

00:04:08.955 --> 00:04:09.788
I mean this in the nicest way possible,

00:04:09.788 --> 00:04:11.670
Dad, I'm sorry, but

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:13.950
my dad needs help like
turning his iPad on.

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:15.510
We'll take it slow and...

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:18.510
- After 10 years of toil,

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:20.070
Kai is understandably proud

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:23.070
that his business finally
has a product to sell.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:23.903
Cool.
- After you.

00:04:23.903 --> 00:04:27.000
Today, we've got one of
our Mark II prototypes,

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.940
production units of the Mark smart gun.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:31.860
So as soon as I start to interact with it,

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.780
I'm going to see those white lights.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:35.490
The white lights basically
mean that it's active.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:37.290
It sees that somebody's picked it up

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:38.640
but it hasn't recognized a user yet,

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.230
so it's still locked

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:41.250
- To set up the gun,

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:44.130
you register your face and
fingerprints on this device

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:45.480
in less than a minute,

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:48.153
where you can also add
and delete other users.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:59.013
If you're not registered, nothing.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.340
- Fingerprints are very well-optimized,

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:03.173
very commercially mature.

00:05:03.173 --> 00:05:04.530
You don't need a lot of processing power

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:05.790
to run them appropriately.

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:07.740
Facial recognition is a
cutting edge biometric,

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.810
which means it requires
orders of magnitude

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:10.643
more processing power.

00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:12.540
So where previously we
were able to just use

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.070
like little microcontrollers
and things like that

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:14.970
to do fingerprints,

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:17.310
we've had to basically
build an entire computer

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:18.360
hardened for the system,

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:19.710
you know, that works inside of a gun

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:20.790
to be able to run facial recognition

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:22.790
at the speeds that our customers demand.

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:26.280
- I've shot guns before

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:29.070
but I'm far from a gun enthusiast.

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:32.280
In many ways, I'm
Biofire's target audience,

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:34.530
a dad who wants to keep his family safe

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:36.600
but who also thinks having a gun at home

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:39.093
probably ends up more dangerous than good.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:43.830
Still, old gun or new,
I needed a few pointers.

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:45.090
- I would try to lean forward,

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:46.950
like just like lean into it a little bit.

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:48.100
Ah, yeah, there you go.

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:52.343
- Okay.

00:05:52.343 --> 00:05:53.520
- And again, the target's
like less than 10 feet away

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:55.534
so you don't need to worry
too much about aiming.

00:05:55.534 --> 00:05:57.354
- Let's not point that out.

00:05:57.354 --> 00:06:00.659
- Sorry, the target is
like a 100-plus yards away.

00:06:00.659 --> 00:06:02.010
And Ashlee is about to, you know, do a

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:04.410
marksman level sort a shot here.

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:06.060
- Am I cool to try it?

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:06.893
- Yup.

00:06:11.844 --> 00:06:12.677
- Whoo

00:06:12.677 --> 00:06:13.510
- There you go.

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:16.050
- It still had more kick

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:17.610
than I thought it was going to.

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:18.930
- It's all good.

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:21.873
- It kicks, and from my
experience, it works.

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:26.673
The question is, will it sell?

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:30.630
Several companies have tried
and failed to make a smart gun

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:32.370
because it's really hard to do,

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:35.100
and not just because you're
mixing sensitive electronics

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:36.990
with regular explosions.

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:38.790
Okay, so you've chosen to do a startup

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:40.620
doing this incredibly hard technology,

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:42.150
and then to wade it into like

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:44.206
a Second Amendment sort of issue, right?

00:06:44.206 --> 00:06:45.039
- Yup.

00:06:45.039 --> 00:06:45.872
- So this is like nightmare.

00:06:45.872 --> 00:06:48.030
- Yeah, keeps it interesting.

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:50.580
- That, as Kai has a talent for doing,

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:52.980
is putting the controversy mildly.

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:55.620
Pro-gun folks hate these
things because they fear

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:58.140
that one day the government
will use the existence

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:01.200
of a working smart gun
to ban the dumb ones.

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:02.760
Even flirting with smart guns

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:05.670
leads to death threats
and financial risks.

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:07.890
One of the reasons Biofire's investors

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:09.690
prefer to stay anonymous,

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:12.180
and plenty of anti-gun
folks don't like them

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:15.210
because, you know, they're guns.

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:17.760
For his part, Kai's not
interested in mandating

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:20.190
or even advertising his product.

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:23.580
Biofire, he says, is just
here to provide choices

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:26.010
and maybe save lives.

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:27.660
- The key thing that
we are trying to solve,

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:29.130
which I think we could completely solve,

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:32.193
is children and teenagers
getting access to firearms.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:35.340
Firearms are now the
leading cause of death

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:36.510
for children and teenagers in America.

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:38.130
They surpassed motor vehicle accidents

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:39.723
for the first time in 2020.

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:42.330
I've never talked to a gun owner who

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:43.620
wanted their kid to find their gun.

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:46.320
And so, we think that by
building technology that,

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:48.570
you know, basically
works with the gun owner

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:50.670
to enforce what they're
already looking to do

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:53.910
allows us to really actually
address that portion of the

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:56.670
challenge of basically
gun deaths in America.

00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:58.710
- The final version of Biofire's gun

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:02.070
arrived in April with
this funky sci-fi look.

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:05.550
It's definitely more Halo
than Red Dead Redemption,

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:08.880
but how well it will
sell is anyone's guess.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:10.560
- We want it to look modern and futuristic

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:11.520
and a little different

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:13.980
than everything else that's
on the market obviously.

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:15.150
- When I saw that one,

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:16.830
it does have a little
sci-fi thing going on.

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:18.630
- Yeah, and again, we don't
want it to look too normal

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:20.700
but we don't want it to
look like only, you know,

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:22.290
something that a sci-fi
nerd would like like myself.

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:23.550
- Like the Cybertruck or something?

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:25.112
- Yeah, I was trying to not say that.

00:08:36.123 --> 00:08:37.740
- Have you ever had that thing, man,

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:39.540
where you're hanging out on a bench

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:40.740
and looking at the clouds

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:43.383
and you're like, "There's a dragon.

00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:46.350
There's a turtle.

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:48.267
Hey, there's a jellyfish."

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:53.100
Wait, I think that jellyfish
has a camera on it.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:55.053
I think that jellyfish is watching me.

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:57.060
It is.

00:08:57.060 --> 00:08:57.893
It is.

00:08:57.893 --> 00:09:00.390
- There is a suspected Chinese spy balloon

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:03.390
that's been hovering
over the northern U.S.

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:04.740
- Yes, there was that moment

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:06.450
that the world looked transfixed

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:08.760
upon our visitor from China.

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:11.160
What China didn't know
though was that America

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:14.220
has its own super high
tech balloon program,

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:17.340
able to be launched by
hoodie-wearing dudes

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:19.383
wherever they can find parking.

00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:25.230
- We saw a path to finally
make the stratosphere

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:26.790
and stratospheric balloons viable,

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:30.060
this super old technology
that has never really been

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:31.910
able to be become broadly applicable.

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:36.960
- The makers of this fancy balloon,

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:39.843
Andrew and Jared, work here at Urban Sky.

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:43.290
The basic idea is that
high altitude balloons

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:46.200
can be used to capture lots
of imagery of the world,

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:48.090
filling the stratosphere-sized gap

00:09:48.090 --> 00:09:50.130
between other aerial options.

00:09:50.130 --> 00:09:53.220
You've got drones at one
layer and planes at one layer

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:55.710
and then there's this like
huge jump up to satellites

00:09:55.710 --> 00:09:57.690
and there's just kind of
like nothing in between.

00:09:57.690 --> 00:09:58.920
- Yeah, I mean we say it sometimes

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:00.780
like we somewhat view ourselves

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:03.510
as like the SpaceX of the stratosphere.

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:07.110
- Urban Sky's images might look
a lot like satellite imagery

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:09.330
but they're much, much more detailed

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:12.270
and can be taken more
cheaply and frequently,

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:14.160
tracking things like wildfires,

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:15.750
the effects of climate change,

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:18.180
and all manner of human activity.

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:20.370
- You can see cars, you can
see features on the roofs,

00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:21.630
you can see landscaping,

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:25.380
you can see light posts, power lines.

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:26.310
- These people are worried.

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:27.804
It's not like espionage faces.

00:10:27.804 --> 00:10:28.637
- You cannot see a person.

00:10:28.637 --> 00:10:30.450
Right, yeah, you cannot see a person.

00:10:30.450 --> 00:10:31.830
We have no intention of doing that.

00:10:31.830 --> 00:10:32.663
- Yeah.

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:36.813
Both men have serious
history with balloons.

00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:40.260
They used to work for World View

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:42.570
which makes massive stratospheric balloons

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:44.730
and also takes pictures with them.

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:47.160
The problem though is that
the balloons are expensive

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:50.670
and take many hours and a
ton of people to launch.

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:54.360
Urban Sky is trying to make
ballooning quick and easy.

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:57.150
- Our big bet was that
sensors and components

00:10:57.150 --> 00:10:58.830
would miniaturize to the
point that we could make

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:01.650
very powerful high
resolution imaging systems

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:03.900
in a very lightweight package.

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:06.180
- I know the first time I
described this company to someone,

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:07.803
they're going to be like, "How
do they control this thing?

00:11:07.803 --> 00:11:11.010
It's just going to fall in
some city on somebody's head."

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:13.440
You know, I mean, it's
pretty obvious concern.

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:16.530
- Yeah, I mean, at a high
level, we do control it.

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:18.390
So like, it's not a balloon
that's just going to fall

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:21.000
out of the sky and go somewhere random.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:24.150
We know pretty precisely
exactly what altitude

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:25.950
our balloon will float at.

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:26.940
Then, you can think of it like

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:28.500
where in like the currents of the ocean,

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:30.150
like they're fairly uniform,

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:31.860
that we're moving in a general direction

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:34.470
when we get to a float altitude.

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:35.760
- When it's time to come down,

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:37.650
Mission Control sends a signal

00:11:37.650 --> 00:11:40.500
and the balloon gently deflates.

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:42.660
- Nice easy landing.

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:44.060
System's in great condition.

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:49.500
- All right, this is the
balloon-making operation.

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:51.077
- Welcome to the balloon
manufacturing, yeah.

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:55.290
- Yes, they're willing to
show us their balloons,

00:11:55.290 --> 00:11:57.300
just not tell us what they're made of.

00:11:57.300 --> 00:11:59.724
- Is it like a plastic or?

00:11:59.724 --> 00:12:01.650
- Yeah, we can speak to it a little bit.

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:04.470
Some of it is a secret in
terms of what it is, but...

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:05.386
- Can I touch it?

00:12:05.386 --> 00:12:06.488
- Yeah.
- Yeah, go for it.

00:12:07.535 --> 00:12:09.540
- It feels light, which it turns out

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:13.050
is important for a balloon
and everything attached to it.

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:14.640
So this is the moneymaker.

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:15.510
- This is the moneymaker.

00:12:15.510 --> 00:12:17.010
This is one of the moneymakers.

00:12:17.010 --> 00:12:19.440
So we have several
different imaging payloads

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:20.535
that we operate.

00:12:20.535 --> 00:12:23.520
This entire thing you see here
weighs less than six pounds.

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:25.560
This is a wildfire monitoring system,

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:29.190
so the guts of this is basically
a thermal infrared camera.

00:12:29.190 --> 00:12:33.540
So NASA and the Air Force
have funded this system,

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:35.250
and we think it'll have a ton of value

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:38.550
for doing early detection of wildfires.

00:12:38.550 --> 00:12:41.370
- And in true Colorado
nature-loving fashion,

00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:43.350
they also want to make it reusable.

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:45.390
- It was always a little
bit heartbreaking to see

00:12:45.390 --> 00:12:48.330
this literal mountain of
thousands of pounds of plastic

00:12:48.330 --> 00:12:49.410
get put in a dumpster.

00:12:49.410 --> 00:12:51.180
- What's the most you've reused one?

00:12:51.180 --> 00:12:52.950
- Three times for one balloon.

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:55.710
So yeah, we can get several
reuses out of a balloon.

00:12:55.710 --> 00:12:57.270
- Eventually, we want
to get to the paradigm

00:12:57.270 --> 00:12:58.680
where these systems are reusable

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:01.050
the way something like
a parachute is reusable,

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:04.230
where you can use the same
system hundreds of times.

00:13:04.230 --> 00:13:05.640
- Okay, let's see this thing

00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:07.650
in its natural habitat.

00:13:07.650 --> 00:13:09.063
Time for a field trip.

00:13:12.802 --> 00:13:16.890
So we found this kind of like
empty little carve out of area

00:13:16.890 --> 00:13:19.920
but you guys could just be
on the side of the road or?

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:21.750
- If it's safe and legal to
park, we can launch from there.

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:22.680
- Yeah, you kind of just need

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:24.360
whatever's as big as the truck, right?

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:25.260
- Right.

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:26.103
Ready to fill.

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:39.660
All right, here we go.

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:43.920
- Is there's something you say?

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:47.280
Up, up and away?

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:48.113
Oh, wow.

00:13:53.900 --> 00:13:54.930
Is it going to inflate?

00:13:54.930 --> 00:13:56.940
Like, it look like a big teardrop or?

00:13:56.940 --> 00:13:59.250
- It is, so it's only
about 10% full right now.

00:13:59.250 --> 00:14:00.083
- Okay.

00:14:00.083 --> 00:14:01.260
- So that gas is expanding

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:02.430
as it gets higher and higher

00:14:02.430 --> 00:14:04.923
and the atmosphere gets
less and less dense.

00:14:10.806 --> 00:14:12.600
- As the balloon went up and up

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:14.760
like a memory from childhood,

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:18.060
I was dangerously close to
feeling something intimate

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:20.583
about a piece of inflated plastic.

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:27.093
I don't know, probably
just the mountain air.

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:38.100
If you're in Denver itself,

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:41.853
it's all beer and pot
shops and dumb bridges,

00:14:44.460 --> 00:14:47.523
tossing some dogs and
joggers, and you get the idea.

00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:52.803
Of course what you want to do
is get to the great outdoors.

00:14:56.820 --> 00:14:59.867
I mean, this might as well
be me heading to Mordor.

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:05.163
It's weird and beautiful, and
it's everywhere in Colorado.

00:15:09.090 --> 00:15:10.590
Colorado's the kind of place

00:15:10.590 --> 00:15:12.540
where you can put on some waders,

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:16.023
hop in a stream, and run
into a rocket scientist.

00:15:17.520 --> 00:15:19.230
Meet Joe Laurienti,

00:15:19.230 --> 00:15:22.503
the CEO of rocket engine
maker, Ursa Major.

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:26.763
- We are on the Big Thompson River,

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:31.530
pretty in the middle of nowhere
as far as Colorado towns go.

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:37.230
This is pretty normal
Colorado fishing experience

00:15:37.230 --> 00:15:38.370
that you drive to these rivers,

00:15:38.370 --> 00:15:39.780
find a spot to pull off the road,

00:15:39.780 --> 00:15:41.880
and go find fish, yeah.

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:44.190
- And what are we going to try to catch?

00:15:44.190 --> 00:15:45.300
What's our goal?

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:47.127
- Just some trout. All right.

00:15:47.127 --> 00:15:49.077
- All right, man, I'm excited.

00:15:50.850 --> 00:15:51.900
- We just want to cast this out

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:54.330
and kind of let the river take it.

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:57.090
- But otherwise the bob's staying on top.

00:15:57.090 --> 00:15:58.770
- Yeah, just pull it
out like that, exactly

00:15:58.770 --> 00:16:00.510
because you want that yellow line

00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:01.960
to go all the way to the end.

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:05.460
So just do that a couple more times, yeah.

00:16:06.570 --> 00:16:08.310
And don't be afraid to have too much line.

00:16:08.310 --> 00:16:11.270
- I just don't want to hit you.

00:16:12.660 --> 00:16:14.210
- There you go, that's perfect.

00:16:16.110 --> 00:16:17.710
- Don't lie to me, Joe.

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:21.954
- This is 30 minutes from the office, so.

00:16:21.954 --> 00:16:23.841
You don't get that in
a lot of other places.

00:16:23.841 --> 00:16:26.713
- Yeah.

00:16:26.713 --> 00:16:28.500
And is it, like, changing

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:31.050
as all that Californians
are coming and ...

00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:33.397
- I don't tend to notice.

00:16:33.397 --> 00:16:36.570
They come help us build rocket engines.

00:16:36.570 --> 00:16:37.403
- Yeah.

00:16:39.330 --> 00:16:40.470
All right, watch your face.

00:16:40.470 --> 00:16:41.303
- All right.

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:47.490
- It's also low risk with
these small trout flies

00:16:47.490 --> 00:16:49.890
'cause the hook is like centimeter long.

00:16:49.890 --> 00:16:52.833
- Yeah, I'd still feel
bad if I hooked a CEO.

00:17:00.150 --> 00:17:03.990
Ursa Major does its work
here in a suburb of Denver,

00:17:03.990 --> 00:17:07.293
where it can fire its big loud engines.

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:11.010
- Where we're looking right now is our,

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:12.360
that's two Hadley engines facing us,

00:17:12.360 --> 00:17:13.980
so we'll see fire coming right at us.

00:17:13.980 --> 00:17:14.813
- Okay.

00:17:14.813 --> 00:17:15.646
- Let's see some fire.

00:17:15.646 --> 00:17:16.479
- Cool.

00:17:18.330 --> 00:17:21.570
- That'll loosen up some seals.

00:17:21.570 --> 00:17:24.113
- The world today is
full of rocket startups,

00:17:25.770 --> 00:17:28.140
and each one is trying
to make their own rockets

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:30.570
and their own rocket engines.

00:17:30.570 --> 00:17:35.347
But as many of them have
learned, engines blow up.

00:17:35.347 --> 00:17:38.373
- Okay, you just saw a bright flash there.

00:17:39.489 --> 00:17:41.933
- It's what makes
building rockets so hard.

00:17:43.290 --> 00:17:46.417
Ursa would like them
to consider a new idea,

00:17:46.417 --> 00:17:48.600
"Don't bother making engines.

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:50.610
Buy them from us."

00:17:50.610 --> 00:17:51.540
Like I've noticed though,

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:54.450
all these rocket companies
I go to, there is this,

00:17:54.450 --> 00:17:57.780
the engine is like it's
like the sexiest part.

00:17:57.780 --> 00:17:58.613
It's like-
- It's romantic, yeah.

00:17:58.613 --> 00:17:59.970
- Yeah, and it's like we're doing

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:01.230
something big and important,

00:18:01.230 --> 00:18:03.810
and everybody wants to
make their own engine

00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:08.040
even though it causes all this
suffering and financial ruin.

00:18:08.040 --> 00:18:11.850
How do you sort of convince
people to get off that?

00:18:11.850 --> 00:18:13.680
- A piece of it is kind of breaking down

00:18:13.680 --> 00:18:15.423
that romantic notion.

00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:21.840
There are a few reasons
companies buy engines from us.

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:24.158
The most common or the easiest sale is

00:18:24.158 --> 00:18:26.340
the make versus buy decision.

00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:28.110
If a company is just
starting, they need an engine.

00:18:28.110 --> 00:18:29.250
We now have engines available.

00:18:29.250 --> 00:18:30.663
That's almost a no-brainer.

00:18:31.650 --> 00:18:32.820
We can save them five years,

00:18:32.820 --> 00:18:35.580
tens or hundreds of millions of dollars.

00:18:35.580 --> 00:18:38.550
The other opportunity is
if there's been a failure,

00:18:38.550 --> 00:18:39.510
if you might have...

00:18:39.510 --> 00:18:41.580
Maybe it's your first flight,
maybe it's your 50th flight,

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:42.750
if you have a launch failure,

00:18:42.750 --> 00:18:44.790
and you're facing down
another five-year engine

00:18:44.790 --> 00:18:46.110
development campaign to fix that.

00:18:46.110 --> 00:18:47.610
- Right, 'cause if there's a failure,

00:18:47.610 --> 00:18:49.230
you have to shut down and
figure out what went wrong.

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:50.063
- Exactly.
- Then come back

00:18:50.063 --> 00:18:51.717
and prove that the fix works.

00:18:55.440 --> 00:18:56.820
- It's almost an easier story to say

00:18:56.820 --> 00:18:58.770
there's a known reliable
engine on the market

00:18:58.770 --> 00:19:00.570
that we're switching to versus

00:19:00.570 --> 00:19:02.620
diving back into why did our engine fail.

00:19:04.200 --> 00:19:07.500
- Is there a fear that the
people who are going to make it

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:08.850
have already kind of made it

00:19:08.850 --> 00:19:10.830
and they're already
making their own engines,

00:19:10.830 --> 00:19:14.130
and you sort of need these
newcomer rocket companies

00:19:14.130 --> 00:19:15.660
that hadn't already made that commitment

00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:17.070
and maybe it's too late?

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:19.380
- I think I would have that fear if

00:19:19.380 --> 00:19:21.660
NASA and the Air Force and the Space Force

00:19:21.660 --> 00:19:23.610
and every commercial
company we're saying today

00:19:23.610 --> 00:19:26.100
we are completely happy with
how we're getting to orbit

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:27.480
and capacity is completely met,

00:19:27.480 --> 00:19:29.283
but that's absolutely not the case.

00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:33.510
- The company is off to a good start

00:19:33.510 --> 00:19:35.640
with big orders from several startups,

00:19:35.640 --> 00:19:37.593
plus some deals with the Air Force.

00:19:38.550 --> 00:19:41.580
They sell a relatively
small rocket engine, Hadley,

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:43.950
for just under a million dollars.

00:19:43.950 --> 00:19:47.670
The next size up, Ripley,
is still in development.

00:19:47.670 --> 00:19:51.540
- This is a turbo pump
assembly for Ripley.

00:19:51.540 --> 00:19:53.370
- Can we, for a moment,

00:19:53.370 --> 00:19:56.940
talk nerd out on turbo pumps?

00:19:56.940 --> 00:19:59.610
Yes, it's true, I'm an engine geek.

00:19:59.610 --> 00:20:03.270
But objectively, Ursa is one
of those badass companies

00:20:03.270 --> 00:20:05.460
with badass hardware.

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:07.525
- The turbo pump really powers the engine.

00:20:07.525 --> 00:20:10.050
It's the heart of the engine.

00:20:10.050 --> 00:20:12.360
Really, you're taking things
from one extreme to another.

00:20:12.360 --> 00:20:15.720
So you're taking minus
300 degree liquid oxygen,

00:20:15.720 --> 00:20:18.480
you're combusting it to
thousands of degrees,

00:20:18.480 --> 00:20:20.250
you're using that to drive
a turbine that needs to go

00:20:20.250 --> 00:20:22.800
from zero to tens of thousands of RPMs.

00:20:22.800 --> 00:20:26.070
- But like even SpaceX for years

00:20:26.070 --> 00:20:27.540
didn't build its own turbo pump.

00:20:27.540 --> 00:20:28.695
They had a company build it.

00:20:28.695 --> 00:20:31.470
- We've got an incredible
turbo machinery team.

00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:33.603
- All made by badass people.

00:20:34.830 --> 00:20:39.660
- I'm a big fan of paragliding,
skydiving, and BASE jumping.

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:42.410
- Like propulsion
technician, Margo Vriesinga.

00:20:44.100 --> 00:20:46.290
- I love the way this company operates.

00:20:46.290 --> 00:20:50.220
I think it's so, it makes so much sense.

00:20:50.220 --> 00:20:53.853
The way that we exist allows
other companies to exist.

00:20:54.780 --> 00:20:56.880
It's almost funny that it
hasn't happened sooner.

00:20:56.880 --> 00:20:58.260
Because you're looking at companies

00:20:58.260 --> 00:20:59.640
like Pratt and Whitney or Rolls-Royce,

00:20:59.640 --> 00:21:00.473
they're making engines.

00:21:00.473 --> 00:21:02.610
Boeing's not making their own engines.

00:21:02.610 --> 00:21:03.443
That would be crazy.

00:21:03.443 --> 00:21:04.920
That's time-consuming.

00:21:04.920 --> 00:21:06.470
That's the hard part, you know?

00:21:07.410 --> 00:21:09.870
- You know, technology
overall is notoriously

00:21:09.870 --> 00:21:11.160
kind of skews male.

00:21:11.160 --> 00:21:13.260
And in my experience,
aerospace companies are

00:21:13.260 --> 00:21:14.917
it's like 9 to 1.

00:21:14.917 --> 00:21:16.627
- Yeah.

00:21:16.627 --> 00:21:19.350
- Yeah, is that weird
or is that changing or?

00:21:19.350 --> 00:21:22.020
- There's definitely
significantly more women here

00:21:22.020 --> 00:21:23.520
than I'm used to working with.

00:21:24.660 --> 00:21:26.880
It's really nice actually
'cause I don't feel like

00:21:26.880 --> 00:21:30.431
I'm like in a fishbowl or
some kind of zoo animal.

00:21:32.280 --> 00:21:34.170
- And of course for practical reasons,

00:21:34.170 --> 00:21:35.433
it has to be here.

00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:38.210
This facility is pretty interesting

00:21:38.210 --> 00:21:41.070
because most of the places I've been,

00:21:41.070 --> 00:21:44.940
if you think about SpaceX
or Blue Origin as examples,

00:21:44.940 --> 00:21:48.630
you're building engines in
California or Washington

00:21:48.630 --> 00:21:50.190
and then you're going all the way to Texas

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:51.930
to do most of the tests.

00:21:51.930 --> 00:21:54.477
You don't have this kind of feedback.

00:21:54.477 --> 00:21:57.281
It just, it slows everything down.

00:21:57.281 --> 00:21:58.160
We just saw it.

00:21:58.160 --> 00:21:59.850
I mean, you can do everything

00:21:59.850 --> 00:22:02.310
in just like a few hundred
yards from each other.

00:22:02.310 --> 00:22:03.663
- Yeah, it's very unique.

00:22:05.700 --> 00:22:07.200
We definitely could not fire engines

00:22:07.200 --> 00:22:08.447
like you saw today in Los Angeles.

00:22:08.447 --> 00:22:10.620
- Yeah.

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:14.643
Because in Colorado you
can be as loud as you like.

00:22:38.238 --> 00:22:39.660
Holy .

00:22:39.660 --> 00:22:41.272
That was like a lot better than,

00:22:41.272 --> 00:22:42.721
I mean, I've seen a lot of time.

00:22:42.721 --> 00:22:44.131
- It's a unique experience out here.

00:22:44.131 --> 00:22:45.540
- Holy cow, man.

00:22:45.540 --> 00:22:47.277
I mean, that was so freaking loud.

00:22:47.277 --> 00:22:50.370
Because even when I've seen
launches, you're a mile away.

00:22:50.370 --> 00:22:52.329
- Yeah, we're like 200 yards.

00:22:52.329 --> 00:22:57.329
- Yeah, that was cool.

00:22:57.520 --> 00:23:00.813
And with that, I did Colorado
the way you're supposed to.

00:23:02.580 --> 00:23:03.773
Guns on the way in,

00:23:04.620 --> 00:23:07.233
glorious fire spigot on the way out.

00:23:08.880 --> 00:23:10.980
Adam Kline in Golden Shoulders,

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:13.089
please play us off.

00:23:13.089 --> 00:23:18.089
♪ I moved away from Denver, Colorado ♪

00:23:18.454 --> 00:23:23.426
♪ And I was all alone ♪

00:23:23.426 --> 00:23:27.532
♪ Alone, alone, alone ♪

00:23:27.532 --> 00:23:30.727
♪ All alone ♪

00:23:30.727 --> 00:23:33.218
♪ All alone ♪

00:23:33.218 --> 00:23:35.662
♪ And I made a huge mistake ♪

00:23:35.662 --> 00:23:40.662
♪ I moved away from Denver, Colorado ♪

00:23:41.730 --> 00:23:45.009
♪ And I don't know why, I don't know why ♪

00:23:45.009 --> 00:23:50.009
♪ I moved away from Denver, Colorado ♪

00:23:50.446 --> 00:23:53.613
♪ And I was all alone ♪

